> On Tue, 29 Apr 2025 at 17:10, Shu Fai Cheung (張樹輝) <shufai...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > Thank you, Jeremy, for sharing the history. They are interesting stories for students to learn about how researchers did SEM then. The go-for-lunch-and-come-back part brings back the good old memories of my grad school life. And we really "hoped" ... because it (LISREL) was sometimes still running after a one-hour lunch. :)
> >
>
> Who remembers another fun one?  Put AM on the OU line, go home, come back the next day and you'd either have a model that fit, or a model that was not identified
>
> In Lisrel, AM stood for 'auto-modify'. It would run the model, find the highest modification index, free that parameter, run the model again, then  free the parameter associated with the new highest modification index, and so on until (IIRC) chi-square was not statistically significant.
>
> Perhaps it's a good thing that lavaan doesn't have that option. :)
>
>
>
> > Jeremy already gave a very good explanation of the matrices. I will share how to get those matrices from lavaan. The lavInspect() function can be used to print the matrices used in lavaan. If used only with the lavaan object, it prints the matrices with the parameter IDs. If called with "est" as the second argument, it prints the estimates.
>
> Ooh, seeing some of those matrices gave me PTSD :). Young people today don't know how easy they have it. Now get off my lawn!
>
> Jeremy
>
>
